### tests/fm-session-lock-ancestry.test.sh (head) — exits at a PRE-EXISTING host assumption
    (unchanged from base: an orphaned pty-host is expected to reparent to pid 1; this sandbox reparents to subreaper 1310)
ok - session-lock: a version-named Claude Code session is identified from its install path and argv[0]
ok - session-lock: a harness that is pid 1 of its own namespace is examined, not skipped
ok - session-lock: ordinary script paths under a harness directory are not harness processes
ok - session-lock: ownership stops at the first non-harness gap above the contiguous run
ok - session-lock: a live version-named session holding the lock is not mistaken for a stale owner
ok - session-lock: a trusted same-session id keeps owning a recycled background chain, and nothing weaker does
ok - session-lock: a trusted id anchors the lock on the model-loop process, anything else on the outermost pid
ok - session-lock: a Windows session is identified from its published pid across the severed parent link
ok - session-lock: a published Windows pid is confirmed against the process table before it is trusted
ok - session-lock: a tagged Windows pid is never resolved against the Cygwin process table
ok - session-lock: a published identity is accepted by every gate that reads the lock
ok - session-lock: a live tagged lock holder is a live foreign owner to a second Windows session
ok - session-lock: fm-lock.sh protects valid tagged owners and reclaims dead or malformed records
ok - session-lock: a published Windows Claude pid is accepted only with its session id
ok - session-lock: a Windows /clear keeps the lock and re-keys its session id
ok - session-lock: a Windows-tagged lock stays inert off Windows
ok - session-lock: a task lease follows a live tagged lock holder and goes stale when it exits
ok - session-lock: a harness in the local process table resolves untagged when ps has no -o option
ok - session-lock e2e: a version-named session claims the home and arms supervision
ok - session-lock e2e: a session parented by a harness-named daemon claims the home and arms supervision
ok - session-lock e2e: a version-named session under a harness-named daemon keeps its own lock
